#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221200 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2e37d37-007c-569b-a44c-cb6a9d075371" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "669126b0-2995-549c-9a80-888bccd3acc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21845" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function\n$F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$\ndefined on $[-5,5).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension of $G(x)=5x$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](images/64/64e7a907f9d039e0b8a9ff71ba4bab18a9325a8322552cda69391e239bf3339f.svg)\nSince $f$ is ![](images/9c/9c95f62579d17c626e85bd76ba0028b378e8a0a3c2b9879d8e596bd9773eea84.svg)\nand  ![](images/90/90d690278324e4611fb59fa60aae82457d5ee10e595057bdd9d9f0e8a6a9db93.svg)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](images/68/689e50221843f953a679c91852b2cc11b4b89cb8aa86613be59fcec65ac75c43.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6}\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous} }$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{107}{6})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-50(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221201 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/id #uuid "6e45e60d-7c68-5c49-8ca2-5381af39cd47" :question/math-academy-id "e-21846" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a41865c9f4c1ea90ac559af16948e7af546a4ad3842adf954f4d8227c3583ab6.svg)\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x \\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\nI. $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\nII. $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\nIII. $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\nIV. $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1cc57825a318df088ac0bc28ffe75c21579c5fe007fbb52cf0710a1937dc1e.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is true since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5fb59869184a0c54ddaeea0b434104f3335c652c3f7533cd9dec0dcdcc91876.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221202 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e01d04-6c13-52eb-a4b6-ad9a5cde8134" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "77c71e9d-c2a4-5a2c-9c67-a90e8913ba13" :question/math-academy-id "e-21856" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\dfrac{3}{4}x^2$ defined on $[-4,4).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{4}x^3 = \\int_0^x f(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](images/ca/cad855e780f39df95e18c8c89e1994a1f60e5609bae77be32d779c2a37dfb7c5.svg)\n Since $f$ is \n![](images/ec/ec188620716be3a7ba759589ec331eb4a2b0b8c522b45382f535889d1c980760.svg)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](images/62/627f5788ec8cf06c5cf32245f8128edba2de2d5941f1e4ffcdc61645ab97c27d.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n \\geq 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{[-4t]}_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3} & ∼-4x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{192(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1124e7b9-2d5f-5e41-894c-4012a241cbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425553} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0eac61-78a6-5e61-9741-36c63bed5514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1124e7b9-2d5f-5e41-894c-4012a241cbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425553} :answer-field/id #uuid "02397001-5967-5501-a894-ba22a52ed078" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425642} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eb5b194-82d3-514f-a7ba-8de2203f7d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425588} {:answer/id #uuid "a0bab576-a7aa-5d44-bb81-8dddab1acc90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eb5b194-82d3-514f-a7ba-8de2203f7d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425588} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f4ed93-3433-5da6-8034-8da63e770508" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425653} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "134b90c6-da6f-564c-b88c-9cde9a9b37af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425556} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa2deb5-9607-580c-9d2a-b98702d2433d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "134b90c6-da6f-564c-b88c-9cde9a9b37af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425556} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9d9c24a-7b11-59ec-a4a0-6136392a9411" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425665} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "930eef55-0159-5954-a696-d7ed742b7ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425605} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fa35e0-047b-50a2-976e-7044fdf2253d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930eef55-0159-5954-a696-d7ed742b7ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425605} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c6d28a-a8a0-5b03-a2be-c422eefbc276" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46096919-32d4-552d-ad62-e0e1723dd83b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342245" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ {x}^{3}, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a29065f008fac7d3fd32d52b2ddc0b7d9360ffd75475f6c169eeb26582d22d2.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}-4x=-4(-2)=8\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{x}^{3}=(2)^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d750ad2f9ea2a85446b94f7b19009ef74eeea25b4ccd1bf8ff4f408a5df381e2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bdf9085-5e57-5fef-9288-dd2d5b77cfd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425551} {:answer/id #uuid "15b4446a-3837-52de-923e-6861cab63dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15b4446a-3837-52de-923e-6861cab63dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425558} :answer-field/id #uuid "0515496f-04cd-566b-90f7-87a3f4b76092" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425645} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057eb7f3-190a-5da9-a784-33d6a0e88a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425547} {:answer/id #uuid "c884611a-198a-588d-a475-eb02622d1e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c884611a-198a-588d-a475-eb02622d1e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425621} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a027e30-e5db-5ef5-823b-5d5213ffe1be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425647} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2beb1cb1-5d44-5f64-aa49-099923f8b16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425568} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2972fd-47e3-5529-9b6d-d5a3d01b3766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2beb1cb1-5d44-5f64-aa49-099923f8b16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425568} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b781fbf-b13f-5c71-940f-e7a40eb71a60" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c442eea8-eb9b-51fe-9d23-75ab92b8ffe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425618} {:answer/id #uuid "f9060f67-f7b4-5a5d-bfd7-7abf56ccf59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c442eea8-eb9b-51fe-9d23-75ab92b8ffe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425618} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc32ec31-8aab-5e25-95f6-47aed03f7c3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3df34aa2-66f8-53fb-b84c-e23ff9464f43" :question/math-academy-id "q-342264" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ 3, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/7a/7afa339233c7e21eda233f5b67ccf8be9e05fb4ce9484c98eaf59eb790e651ab.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}x=-2\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}3=3.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ac6956ec22cfed2d304cc5c2352cf1fdf4c795ea423e7a19c8e3a3e2dc843ed.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf9be56-efa9-5355-8976-ae2a23fa9c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425552} {:answer/id #uuid "518e2251-cc5d-5108-bce8-18685a7d6e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref